<%#
  views/public/error.ejs

  The one error page for the whole site, used for every error on a normal page
  (API requests get JSON instead): 404 for an unknown URL or an article that isn't
  live, 401/403 for pages someone may not open, 400 for bad input, 500 when
  something breaks on the server. There is no separate 404 view.

  Locals (both required):
    statusCode - the HTTP status, for example 404
    message    - a short sentence a reader can understand, for example "Article not found"

  How a controller renders it:
    res.status(404).render('public/error', { statusCode: 404, message: 'Article not found' })
  Most errors don't render it directly: they call next(error), and
  middleware/errorHandler.js renders this page with the right status.

  Both values are printed with the escaping tag (equals sign), so even if a message
  contained HTML it would be shown as text, never run.

  TEMP until the shared partials (views/partials/head, header, footer) are merged:
  this page is a complete HTML document on its own. When they exist, replace the
  head, header and footer below with includes, so the error page looks like the
  rest of the site.

  Rendered by: middleware/errorHandler.js, and controllers for "not found" cases
  (for example SH's article page).
  Owner: OS.
%>
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8">
  <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1">
  <title><%= statusCode %> - The Web Daily</title>
  <link rel="stylesheet" href="/css/main.css">
</head>
<body>
  <header>
    <a href="/">The Web Daily</a>
  </header>

  <main class="page-container">
    <section class="empty-state" aria-labelledby="error-title">
      <h1 id="error-title">Error <%= statusCode %></h1>
      <p><%= message %></p>
      <p><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/">Back to the home page</a></p>
    </section>
  </main>
</body>
</html>
